Final answer:

```c
static
void kernel_lu(int n,
	       DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(A,N,N,n,n))
{
  int i, j, k;

#pragma scop
  #pragma omp parallel private(i, j, k) shared(A)
  {
    for (i = 0; i < _PB_N; i++) {
      #pragma omp single
      {
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
          for (k = 0; k < j; k++) {
            A[i][j] -= A[i][k] * A[k][j];
          }
          A[i][j] /= A[j][j];
        }
      }
      #pragma omp for schedule(static)
      for (j = i; j < _PB_N; j++) {
        for (k = 0; k < i; k++) {
          A[i][j] -= A[i][k] * A[k][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
#pragma endscop
}
```

This preserves the original semantics while:
1. Using a single persistent parallel region (reduces fork/join overhead)
2. Parallelizing the independent j iterations in the second loop
3. Using static scheduling (uniform work per j iteration)
4. Keeping the sequential dependency in the first loop correct via `single`
5. Maintaining proper synchronization via implicit barriers

